      "fileTexts.normalReadme": "SQL injection and anomalous search results — Normal logs before the attack\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, traffic, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nThese are normal logs before the attack, showing ordinary business and administration activity. The attack button switches to logs with prepared, static training events. No traffic or commands are sent or executed. Restoring normal logs returns to this state.\n\nInvestigation files:\n  /var/log/nginx/access.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.jsonl\n  /var/log/db/query.log\n\nBasic commands: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample: grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filtering and aggregation through pipes. Type help for assistance.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis. It uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observed facts from inferences and corroborate findings across logs.\n\nCompare normal and post-attack states to find added lines and changed processing results. Legitimate input may contain punctuation such as apostrophes.\n\nNginx uses a custom log_format extending combined with request_id. Standard combined does not contain request_id. In a real deployment, log $request_id, pass it to the application with a setting such as proxy_set_header X-Request-ID $request_id, and record the same value in application audit. The readable req-… IDs here are fixed teaching values; native Nginx $request_id produces 32 hexadecimal characters.\n",
      "fileTexts.attackedReadme": "Case 4: Traces of SQL injection\n\nAll organizations, people, accounts, traffic, and logs in this exercise are fictional.\nIP addresses use documentation ranges; domains use .test / .example.\nDate: August 12, 2026. All log timestamps are UTC (Japan time is UTC+9).\n\nCompare normal searches with invalid-input attempts. Do not infer success solely from syntax errors or HTTP 200; verify query structure and row counts in DB and application audits.\n\nInvestigation files:\n  /var/log/nginx/access.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.log\n  /var/log/app/audit.jsonl\n  /var/log/db/query.log\n\nBasic commands: pwd, ls, cd, cat, head, tail, less, grep, awk, cut, sort, uniq, wc, sed\nExample: grep -n \"pattern\" /var/log/example.log\nCombine filtering and aggregation through pipes. Type help for assistance.\nThis terminal simulates log analysis. It uses no real OS or external communication.\nSeparate observed facts from inferences and corroborate findings across logs.\n\naudit.log and audit.jsonl are text and JSON Lines views of the same application events. The query-structure information in query.log is normalized from this exercise's audit measurements. The legitimate O'Brien search is also available for comparison.\n\nNginx uses a custom log_format extending combined with request_id. Standard combined does not contain request_id. In a real deployment, log $request_id, pass it to the application with a setting such as proxy_set_header X-Request-ID $request_id, and record the same value in application audit. The readable req-… IDs here are fixed teaching values; native Nginx $request_id produces 32 hexadecimal characters.\n",
